#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134639} :activity/id #uuid "4c4667e9-24f3-5008-8524-d22f1ffd95c4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049274 :topic/id #uuid "e91f6cfa-b71b-58c3-9a37-a719067b050f" :topic/math-academy-id 2098} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135035 :tutorial/content "Any vector space $V$ combined with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ on $V$ is called an **inner product space.** \n\nWhen referring to inner product spaces, we often say that the vector space $V$ is *equipped* with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$\n\nThe **norm** of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in V$ in an inner product space is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis definition allows us to apply the geometric concept of length to vectors defined in abstract vector spaces. This is because the norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ in an inner product space is a generalization of the norm (or length) of a vector in Euclidean space. To see this, recall that for $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}},\n$$\n\nand we have already seen that the dot product is itself an inner product.\n\nLet's consider an example. Suppose that an inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  3x_1 y_1 + 2x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the vector $\\mathbf v\\in V,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+2\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+8+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9ebd17c5-c7b8-5b1e-9cc0-f09e90d33b65" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ee82987a-98b3-5537-91bb-57a64f18328b" :step/math-academy-id 20197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133576 :question/id #uuid "105a5a4a-2fea-54bb-97c6-c2a700b05f61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364192}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "d483cc70-2180-553f-978b-38be0eded2b4" :step/math-academy-id 20198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133577 :question/id #uuid "5aafdd7d-939a-5690-ac4d-46b554524d75" :question/math-academy-id "e-5549"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364634}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"} :step/id #uuid "84c439ae-b02e-5cc4-a2d2-d32c4de47cf9" :step/math-academy-id 20199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135036 :tutorial/content "Recall that a unit vector is a vector whose length is equal to $1.$ If we divide a non-zero vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb R^n$ by its norm $\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert,$ we obtain a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{x}\\Vert}\n$$\n\nThe process of constructing a unit vector by dividing $\\mathbf x$ by its norm is called **normalization.** \n\nWe normalize vectors in more general inner product spaces using the same method. \n\nFor example, consider an inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + x_3 y_3.$  Let's normalize the following vector with respect to our inner product:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8+3+4 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $\\mathbf v$, we divide $\\mathbf v$ by its norm $\\|\\mathbf v\\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58091751-bbb6-5df0-9dd8-f78f7a9be03a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3607 :tutorial/title "Normalizing a Vector in Inner Product Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "cd671cae-bc1d-5a90-b073-44a44c81c43f" :step/math-academy-id 20200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133578 :question/id #uuid "d279bb1d-754e-5044-9a01-d4c86f30b269" :question/math-academy-id "e-5550"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364641}] :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"} :step/id #uuid "34684ebd-9f9b-5460-9399-460b4b30d52e" :step/math-academy-id 20201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133629 :question/id #uuid "35e7ce1a-b2cc-50dd-8bf7-24d20e55d623" :question/math-academy-id "e-5908"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360800}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "1b7c6d7c-5ecf-5461-8808-91f0fbc8c093" :step/math-academy-id 21783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134640}}] :activity/title "The Norm of a Vector in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133985}]}
